"「アニメシリーズ制作における 制作進行のマニュアル」 日本語版、英語版、中国語（簡体字）版 | 日本動画協会 (https://aja.gr.jp/jigyou/ikusei/%E3%80%8C%E3%82%A2%E3%83%8B%E3%83%A1%E3%82%B7%E3%83%AA%E3%83%BC%E3%82%BA%E5%88%B6%E4%BD%9C%E3%81%AB%E3%81%8A%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B-%E5%88%B6%E4%BD%9C%E9%80%B2%E8%A1%8C%E3%81%AE%E3%83%9E%E3%83%8B%E3%83%A5)\nciteturn17view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view0\",\"pattern\":\".pdf\"}); Total lines: 271\nL168: ### 【50†アニメシリーズ制作における 制作進行のマニュアル】\nL169: \nL170: 1 ファイル 18.43 MB\nL171: \nL172: 【51†ダウンロード】\nL173: \nL174: 【49†Image: アイコン】\nL175: \nL176: ### 【52†A Manual for TV Anime Series Production Assistants アニメシリーズ制作における-制作進行のマニュアル_英語】\nL177: \nL178: 1 ファイル 1.76 MB\nL179: \nL180: 【53†ダウンロード】\nL181: \nL182: 【49†Image: アイコン】\nL183: \nL184: ### 【54†电视动画系列片创作中的制作进行工作指南　アニメシリーズ制作における 制作進行のマニュアル_中国語(簡体字)】\nL185: \nL186: 1 ファイル 1.55 MB\nL187: \nL188: 【55†ダウンロード】\nL189: \nL190: 英語版、中国語（簡体字）版\nL191: 名称：\nL192: 英語版名称「A Manual for TV Anime Series Production Assistants」\nL193: 中国語（簡体字）版名称「电视动画系列片创作中的制作进行工作指南」\nL194: 公開日：2021年3月31日\nL195: 頁数：PDF/51P（英語版）\nL196: PDF/52P（中国語（簡体字））\nL197: 配布：無償配布\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook.pdf (https://mdpi-res.com/bookfiles/book/1386/Japanese_Media_Cultures_in_Japan_and_Abroad_Transnational_Consumption_of_Manga_Anime_and_MediaMixes.pdf?v=1748566890)\nciteturn17view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 164; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view1\",\"pattern\":\"sakuga\"}); Total lines: 7219\nL3179@P76: When these animation production issues are resolved, many times they will display the intradiegetic\nL3180@P76: animation at the end of the episode, the sakuga (a complex animated sequence), often lasting just a few\nL3181@P76: seconds, but revealing the fruits of the labor (and its conflicts) that were just witnessed as the drama\nL3182@P76: of the episode. In this way, the series forces the viewer to focus on the sakuga sequences as the product\nL3183@P76: of labor by many people. These sequences are all from the intradiegetic anime the studio is producing,\nL3184@P76: but one which we (the viewers) do not know the details of the narrative.2 This type of consumption of\nL3185@P76: the animation sequence itself, estranged from narrative, resembles the type of consumption practices\nL3186@P76: employed by a particular type of fan: the sakuga-fan.\nL3187@P76: While I have glossed sakuga above as “a complex animated sequence,” the very term “sakuga”\nL3188@P76: is slightly ambiguous in Japanese. At its most simple, it is “images that are made” and is usually\nL3189@P76: associated with key-animation (officially known by the separate term of genga—key-frames that are\nL3190@P76: not colored and do not include in-betweens) but sakuga can also mean the final cut of an animated\nL3191@P76: sequence. As such, the term implies that it is merely one part of a larger production process and\nL3192@P76: holds the potential for an ambiguity of attribution to multiple agents in its production while still\nL3193@P76: attributing credit to a key-animator. Terminology aside, it is the sakuga part of anime production that\nL3194@P76: is the central concern of sakuga-fans. They are interested in these key sequences as the focal point of\nL3195@P76: production. While not ignoring elements like narrative, character design, or voice-acting, sakuga is\nL3196@P76: what they concentrate on.\nL3197@P76: As such, in the final section, I will transition into how sakuga-fans tend to focus on form and\nL3696@P86: 3. Sakuga-Fans: Activities and Effects\nL3697@P86: 3.1. Focus on Anime’s Media-Form\nL3698@P86: As noted prior, throughout the series, Shirobako directly highlights sakuga animation (e.g., scenes\nL3699@P86: of flipping through key-frames, additions at the end of the episode), forcing a particular type\nL3700@P86: of consumption upon the viewer, making them consume not just a narrative about production that\nL3701@P86: 5 I discuss this in more detail in a forthcoming book chapter “Repeating Anime’s Creativity across Asia” in Trans-Asia as\nL3702@P86: Method: Theory and Practices. Edited by Jeroen de Kloet, Yiu Fai Chow, and Gladys Pak Lei Chong. London: Rowan and\nL3703@P86: Littlefield International.\nL3704@P86-87: 78Arts 2018, 7, 27\nL3705@P87: focuses on the labor “behind” anime but also to acknowledge the actual animation itself, in its sakuga\nL3706@P87: condition. This is the same focus for sakuga-fans, except it is expanded to their engagement with any\nL3707@P87: anime work. In other words, they are explicitly concerned with anime’s media-form, specifically from\nL3708@P87: a technical, production process, and aesthetic perspective. This is evident in the works that sakuga-fans\nL3709@P87: produce, published on the internet. Emblematic of such works is the popular Sakuga Blog, run by\nL3710@P87: a multinational group of English-speaking fans: (using the online pseudonyms of the top 3 writers)\nL3711@P87: kViN is located in Barcelona, Spain; Disgaeamad is located in Tokyo, Japan; and liborek is located\nL3712@P87: in the Czech Republic. Sakuga Blog publishes interviews with animators (conducted by the site or\nL3713@P87: translations from Japanese publications into English, such as a translation of an interview with Megumi\nL3714@P87: Kouno, Disgaeamad 2016), reviews of the production of airing TV shows, and runs a large archive of\nL3715@P87: animation sequences. This archive is called Sakuga Booru, which hosts short sequences of animation\nL3716@P87: (without sound) that are organized and categorized according to anime title and the key-animator\nL3717@P87: that it is credited to. With each animation tagged with that information, the site is easily searchable\nL3718@P87: by either anime title or animator. There are also a number of anime YouTubers that focus on sakuga.\nL3719@P87: They produce videos where they discuss specific animators, showing certain segments, often estranged\nL3720@P87: from their context in the narrative, detailing the production history of that work and/or describing the\nL3721@P87: history of the animators and their work. Some of them have substantial followings, such as AnimeAJay\nL3722@P87: from the UK, who has 136,000 subscribers. While there are other resources by and for fans of sakuga,\nL3723@P87: I will be focusing on these here.\nL3724@P87: It is important to note that these fans are explicitly interested in anime’s media-form and by\nL3725@P87: extension its production process, and through this they become somewhat critically engaged, mainly\nL3726@P87: in concern to the terrible work conditions of the animators themselves. This is something left out of\nL3727@P87: Shirobako, which does not show the dire situation of many of the animators. While it does display\nL3728@P87: the stresses involved in the demanding work and portrays some animators (and other staff) as less\nL3729@P87: financially stable, the series makes no sustained remarks in the narrative on the severity and precarity\nL3730@P87: of their livelihood, or the meager pay of the animators. This is a well-known issue in the anime\nL3731@P87: industry, something that many fans have taken note of, and sakuga-fans are actively critical of the\nL3732@P87: situation. This is particularly evident in the blogs, which decry the animators’ pay and work conditions.\nL3733@P87: Such a position is blatantly evident in a recent post on Sakuga Blog entitled “The Struggle of Anime’s\nL3734@P87: Novice Creators”, which goes into great detail about the difficulties of junior animators, the harsh\nL3735@P87: working conditions, low pay, and poor job security, but also suggests possible alternatives and praises\nL3736@P87: studios like Kyoto Animation, which does seem to provide a favorable (and successful) model for\nL3737@P87: other studios (kViN 2018a).\nL3738@P87: In addition to the direct discussion of their work situation, one might also interpret the focus\nL3739@P87: on animators as an attempt to address this. Sakuga Blog highlights newcomers in the industry, who\nL3740@P87: are in the most precarious position as junior animators, making the least amount of money, lacking\nL3741@P87: experience and contacts. Though not explicitly stated, this praise and promotion works as a way to\nL3742@P87: elevate their status (especially among English-reading fans) and could potentially raise their profile\nL3743@P87: within the industry. As such, sakuga-fans are to a degree politically active, acknowledging the poor\nL3744@P87: working conditions and pay of the animators, actively investigating and disseminating information on\nL3745@P87: the situation, and, in a sense, calling for solutions to these systemic problems. It is their engagement\nL3746@P87: with the media-form that then involved research into the production process, which exposed the uglier\nL3749@P87: role of outsourcing (kViN 2018b). Accompanying this understanding of the integral role of outsourcing\nL3750@P87: in anime production has also led to an acknowledgement of anime’s transnational production, which I\nL3751@P87: will detail below.\nL3752@P87-88: 79Arts 2018, 7, 27\nL3753@P88: 3.2. Engaging Anime’s Transnationality\nL3754@P88: Many of the activities of sakuga-fans function as a form of knowledge production. This includes\nL3755@P88: the blogs that hosts interviews, the videos that expose (and educate) other fans on animators and\nL3756@P88: the production process, and the archiving work that categorizes animators with the sequences\nL3757@P88: they animated. This is an extremely difficult research process, as the credits of an episode do\nL3758@P88: not include which animator drew which sequence. Sakuga YouTuber AnimeAJay, from the UK,\nL3759@P88: describes his method of determining which animators produced which scenes in an extended video\nL3760@P88: (AnimeAjay 2017). He explains how he carefully combs through the credits, cross-referencing the staff\nL3771@P88: directors are supposed to be of primary importance, followed by producers or writers, followed by\nL3772@P88: animation directors, key animators, and character designers” (p. 145). In this sense, building on\nL3773@P88: Lamarre’s observations, we can see that sakuga-fan practices intimately engage with the media-form\nL3774@P88: of anime, flattening received hierarchies of production. This allows for the appreciation of anime\nL3775@P88: as a performance of animation, one that does not necessarily have to be seen as exclusive to Japan.\nL3776@P88: As such, I would suggest that when fans search out the sequences by particular key-animators, they are\nL3777@P88: looking for exceptional performances of anime’s media-form rather than seeing anime as a commentary\nL3778@P88: on Japan (or Japanese society).\nL3779@P88: There is thus an effect of a levelling of the transnationality of anime’s production in the knowledge\nL3780@P88: produced by the sakuga-fans, in which they do not focus on anime’s Japaneseness. This is not\nL3781@P88: necessarily explicitly said but is the effect of the way in which they position non-Japanese animators.\nL3782@P88: While this is present in the archive of Sakuga Booru, which features non-Japanese animators such as\nL3783@P88: Austrian Bahi JD in the same manner as Japanese animators, there are more active approaches that\nL3784@P88: integrate non-Japanese animators as valued instances of anime performance. For example, the Sakuga\nL3785@P88: Blog, conducted a long interview with Korean animator Se Jun Kim, discussing his recent work as\nL3786@P88: a director and his accomplishments as an animator (Kraker2k 2017). This is also present in the YouTube\nL3787@P88: community. Anime YouTuber (that is, not specializing in sakuga, but talking about anime generally)\nL3788@P88: The Canipa Effect began a series called “animator spotlight”, which discusses a particular animator’s\nL3789@P88: work across different anime with Kim as the focus (The Canipa Effect 2015). The very first animator he\nL3790@P88: chose was not Japanese (but works in Japan), and he labels him the “Modern Mecha Master”, adoringly\nL3916@P91: Kraker2k. 2017. Exclusive Interview with Sejoon Kim—Director of Gundam Twilight Axis. Sakuga Blog (Blog).\nL3917@P91: September 7. Available online: https://blog.sakugabooru.com/2017/09/07/interview-with-sejoon-kim/\nL3918@P91: (accessed on 5 May 2018).\nL3919@P91: kViN. 2018a. The Struggle of Anime’s Novice Creators. Sakuga Blog (Blog). March 7. Available online: https:\nL3920@P91: //blog.sakugabooru.com/2018/03/07/the-struggle-of-animes-novice-creators/ (accessed on 5 May 2018).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook.pdf (https://mdpi-res.com/bookfiles/book/1386/Japanese_Media_Cultures_in_Japan_and_Abroad_Transnational_Consumption_of_Manga_Anime_and_MediaMixes.pdf?v=1748566890)\nciteturn17view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 164; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view1\",\"pattern\":\"genga\"}); Total lines: 7219\nL3188@P76: is slightly ambiguous in Japanese. At its most simple, it is “images that are made” and is usually\nL3189@P76: associated with key-animation (officially known by the separate term of genga—key-frames that are\nL3190@P76: not colored and do not include in-betweens) but sakuga can also mean the final cut of an animated\nL3191@P76: sequence. As such, the term implies that it is merely one part of a larger production process and\nL3192@P76: holds the potential for an ambiguity of attribution to multiple agents in its production while still\nL5862@P132: signature shots—seen in most of his early works and frequently used in Sherlock Hound—comes as a\nL5863@P132: result of his collaboration with animator Yasuo Otsuka who is mainly remembered for using the ‘peg ¯\nL5864@P132: hole’ technique. By rotating the still (genga) around the hole at the top of the animation sheet (the peg\nL5865@P132: bar, and thus the name of the technique), Miyazaki and Otsuka set a unique feeling of unbalance. ¯\nL5866@P132: Thus, Miyazaki’s frenzy runners would oscillate into and out of the motion—barely keeping their\n"
